<Fragment set:html={`
<p>The expenditure breakdown varies significantly across countries. China's investment share is unusually high (over 40%) while its consumption share is unusually low (around 55%), reflecting its development model of building infrastructure and manufacturing capacity. Germany runs persistent trade surpluses, so net exports contribute positively; the United States runs deficits, so net exports subtract from GDP.</p>

<h3>The Income Approach (Earning)</h3>

<p>Every dollar spent on output becomes income for someone. The baker who sold you bread uses that revenue to pay wages, rent, and suppliers, with any remainder as profit. The income approach adds up all these payments:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Wages and salaries</strong>: Compensation for labor, including benefits</li>
<li><strong>Rent</strong>: Returns to land and property owners</li>
<li><strong>Interest</strong>: Returns to capital lending</li>
<li><strong>Profits</strong>: Returns to business owners (including corporate profits and proprietors' income)</li>
</ul>

<p>These correspond to the classical "factors of production"—labor, land, capital, and entrepreneurship. Everyone who contributes to production receives some form of income.</p>

<p>The income approach also includes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Depreciation (capital consumption allowance)</strong>: Wear and tear on capital</li>
<li><strong>Indirect taxes minus subsidies</strong>: Taxes like sales taxes are in prices but don't go to factors of production; subsidies are the reverse</li>
</ul>

<p>Added together: <strong>GDP = Wages + Rent + Interest + Profits + Depreciation + (Indirect Taxes - Subsidies)</strong></p>

<p>This must equal expenditure GDP by accounting identity: what buyers spend is exactly what sellers receive. It's the same money flowing in circles—out of purchasers' pockets, through businesses, into workers' and owners' pockets.</p>

<p>The income approach illuminates questions that expenditure GDP cannot answer. What share of income goes to labor versus capital? This has become intensely debated as labor's share has declined in many countries since 1980. Are profits rising because of genuine productivity gains or market power? The income approach provides the data to investigate.</p>

<h3>The Production Approach (Value Added)</h3>

<p>The production approach sums value added across all industries. For each business:</p>

<p><strong>Value Added = Output Value - Intermediate Input Costs</strong></p>

<p>A steel mill buys iron ore for $100 million, electricity for $20 million, and other inputs for $30 million. It produces steel worth $300 million. Value added: $300M - $150M = $150 million. Sum this across every business in the economy, and you get GDP.</p>

<p>This approach directly measures production rather than inferring it from spending or income. It also reveals the sectoral composition of the economy:</p>

<ul>
<li>How much comes from manufacturing versus services?</li>
<li>Is the agricultural sector growing or shrinking?</li>
<li>Which industries are the engines of growth?</li>
</ul>

<p>For the United States, services now dominate—about 77% of GDP—while manufacturing has declined to about 11%. This transformation from a manufacturing economy to a services economy is one of the defining features of modern economic development.</p>

<h3>Why Three Approaches Must Agree</h3>

<p>Imagine a simple economy: a baker bakes bread and sells it.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Expenditure</strong>: You pay $4 for bread → $4 GDP</li>
<li><strong>Income</strong>: Baker receives $4 in revenue, pays it out as wages/profit → $4 GDP</li>
<li><strong>Production</strong>: Baker adds $4 of value → $4 GDP</li>
</ul>

<p>The three approaches are measuring the same underlying reality from different angles. In a more complex economy with millions of transactions, the principle holds: total spending equals total income equals total value added.</p>

<p>In practice, statistical agencies use all three approaches as cross-checks. They won't match perfectly due to measurement error—the U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis publishes a "statistical discrepancy" showing how much the approaches diverge. Large discrepancies signal data problems requiring investigation.</p>

