<p>The United States remains the world's largest economy at about $27 trillion, with China second at around $18 trillion. But this ranking deserves serious scrutiny.</p>

<p>GDP in current dollars is heavily affected by exchange rates. When the dollar strengthens, other countries' GDPs look smaller in dollar terms, even if nothing changed in their domestic economies. Japan's GDP in dollar terms has fluctuated wildly over the decades—not because Japanese production changed that much, but because the yen-dollar exchange rate moved.</p>

<p>Economists often prefer <strong>purchasing power parity (PPP)</strong> adjustments, which account for price differences across countries. A dollar buys more in India than in Switzerland. A haircut costs $5 in Delhi and $50 in Zurich, but it's the same haircut. PPP adjustments try to measure output in terms of what it could actually buy.</p>

<p>On a PPP basis, the picture changes dramatically:</p>
<ul>
<li>China's economy is already larger than America's ($31 trillion vs $27 trillion in PPP terms)</li>
<li>India rises to third place ($14 trillion PPP vs $3.7 trillion nominal)</li>
<li>Developing countries generally look bigger relative to rich countries</li>
</ul>

<p>Neither measure is "correct." Nominal GDP reflects international purchasing power—how much stuff a country can buy on world markets. PPP GDP reflects domestic purchasing power—living standards within the country. Both have uses.</p>

