<p>The gaps are staggering. High-income countries average around $50,000 per person annually; low-income countries average around $1,000. This 50:1 ratio reflects fundamental differences in productivity, technology, infrastructure, institutions, education, and health—the great puzzle that economists call "the wealth and poverty of nations."</p>

<p>Understanding why some countries are rich and others poor is arguably the central question of economics. GDP per capita provides the dependent variable—the thing we're trying to explain—but the explanations involve everything from geography to governance to culture to luck.</p>

<h3>The Averages Hide Reality</h3>

<p>But per capita GDP is still an average, and averages can deceive. The United States has higher per capita GDP than most European countries, but also higher inequality. The "typical" American's experience depends heavily on where they fall in the income distribution.</p>

<p>Consider two hypothetical countries, each with 100 people and $100,000 in total GDP:</p>
<ul>
<li>Country A: Everyone earns $1,000 (GDP per capita = $1,000)</li>
<li>Country B: One person earns $91,000, ninety-nine earn $91 each (GDP per capita = $1,000)</li>
</ul>

<p>Same GDP per capita, radically different lived experiences. Country B's per capita figure is technically accurate but deeply misleading about typical welfare.</p>

<p>Real countries aren't this extreme, but inequality matters. Qatar has the highest GDP per capita in the world (around $85,000), but this average is driven by massive oil wealth that flows primarily to citizens while migrant workers—who constitute 88% of the population—work in very different conditions.</p>

<p>For this reason, economists increasingly supplement GDP per capita with distributional measures:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Median income</strong> (the middle person's income, unaffected by billionaires)</li>
<li><strong>Income share of bottom 40%</strong> (how much goes to the less well-off)</li>
<li><strong>Gini coefficient</strong> (a measure of overall inequality from 0 to 1)</li>
</ul>

<p>These don't replace GDP per capita but provide essential context for interpreting what average output means for actual people.</p>

